# Build context is the repo ROOT (see apps/*/Dockerfile), so this governs every image.
#
# node_modules is the load-bearing entry. The Dockerfiles run a filtered
# `pnpm install` and then `COPY apps/platform apps/platform`, which would copy a
# developer's host node_modules straight over the ones just installed — on Windows
# that lands broken symlinks and the build dies with
#   Cannot find module '/repo/apps/platform/node_modules/next/dist/bin/next'
# CI never hit it because its checkout has no node_modules, so the image built one
# way on a laptop and another way in CI. That difference is exactly what makes a
# Dockerfile impossible to verify before pushing.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Host build output. Copying it in would ship a stale .next and defeat the
# build stage entirely.
.next
**/.next
out
**/out
dist
**/dist
.turbo

# Never bake secrets into an image. .env.example is documentation and harmless,
# but nothing in the build reads it, so it stays out too.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Test and tooling output.
coverage
playwright-report
**/e2e/.results
**/e2e/.state
*.log

# Not needed to build, and .git is large.
.git
.github
.vscode
.idea
docs
images
deploy
*.md
!apps/*/README.md
